Treat requiredSubgroupSizeStages == 0 as unrestricted subgroup sizes.

Device::supports_subgroup_size_log2() ends by asking whether the requested
shader stage appears in VkPhysicalDeviceSubgroupSizeControlProperties::
requiredSubgroupSizeStages. A zero value is defined to mean the implementation
places no stage restriction at all, so the application may pick any subgroup
size for any stage. The check treated zero as "no stage qualifies" and
therefore rejected every non-degenerate request.

MoltenVK reports requiredSubgroupSizeStages == 0, so on macOS every
supports_subgroup_size_log2() query failed and PyroWave's Encoder::init()
refused to create an encoder. PyroWave only accepts a subgroup size of 4, 5 or
6, which is exactly the kind of request this code path rejected.

Returns true only once the existing overlap test above has confirmed the
requested size lies within [minSubgroupSize, maxSubgroupSize], so an
out-of-range size is still rejected. Devices that report a non-zero mask keep
the original behaviour, and the degenerate 0/0 (VARIABLE_SIZE) case already
returned true above.

Needed to make PyroWave usable on macOS. Fix the same inversion upstream.
diff --git a/Granite/vulkan/device.cpp b/Granite/vulkan/device.cpp
--- a/Granite/vulkan/device.cpp
+++ b/Granite/vulkan/device.cpp
@@ -5871,6 +5871,11 @@
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	// A zero requiredSubgroupSizeStages means the implementation imposes no
+	// stage restriction, so any size in the overlap is usable.
+	if (ext.vk13_props.requiredSubgroupSizeStages == 0)
+		return true;
+
 	// We need requiredSubgroupSizeStages support here.
 	return (ext.vk13_props.requiredSubgroupSizeStages & stage) != 0;
 }
